From 5f9d58d14212efc7899591cd07e4b375184c35d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin <j@ukzn>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 02:49:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Use the colours everywhere, and show the rules a standing
 came from

Deyajal was still orange in the app after the last patch because the colours
stopped at the checker: store_to_parsed never read them, so every path that runs
off the captured data - the cohort check, the per-student detail, the badge -
still saw a student with one RISK from 2023 and nothing since.

  - store.colour_rows() gives the colour table in the parser's shape, and
    store_to_parsed hands it over with the results and decisions.
  - _load_history reads a student's standing from the newest COLOUR period, not
    the newest code. A code is written only when something needs saying, so the
    newest one can be years old; reading it as today's standing leaves a student
    flagged at risk long after the registrar put them back on green.
  - student_detail assembles the same inputs the cohort check does, including
    the run period, which it was taking from the student's own last decision -
    so a student whose last code was 2023 was being checked against 2023.
    Verified identical to the cohort row on a sample of 40.
  - check_parsed counts anyone the registrar stated a standing for, colour
    included, so the cohort is the cohort rather than only the coded part of it.
    ENG-CIVIL 2026:1 with no roster: 168 students checked becomes 299.

For seeing why: engine.explain() walks every criterion in order and reports each
rule as passed or failed, and the detail dialog renders it as the path the
student took - the tests they cleared, then the one that applied. The dialog also
shows the full colour history, which standing the registrar stated and whether it
came from a code or the colour block. The ERS tab gains an "All students" list,
searchable, so any student can be opened, not only the disagreements.

Also: a missing rule file made the engine fall back to its built-in defaults,
which carry no progression table. It answered, and the answers were wrong - 88
invented mismatches in a run I made with an unresolvable yaml path. The check now
reports rules_ready and the tab says so plainly instead of showing the fallout as
disagreement.
